Mark XXXI (part 1): the EDITH protocol - grants, ledger, revocation

Default state = ZERO reach. Every reach capability is denied until the
user interactively types "I grant UMBRA full access".

- access.py: the grant lives in access-state.json with a device
  fingerprint and a hard expiry (config access.window_hours, default 24h)
  - it must be re-granted after expiry and never matches another machine.
  Created ONLY by jarvis access grant --full with the typed confirmation
  phrase - never by config files, env vars, CLI flags, API calls, or
  automation. require_full() gates every reach capability and logs the
  attempt either way.
- Tamper-evident ledger: access-log.jsonl with chained SHA-256 entries;
  jarvis access log prints it with integrity verdicts; jarvis access
  status / revoke --all complete the surface.
- Gates wired: jarvis inject install refuses without the grant (--dry-run
  stays available: it is read-only planning with zero reach) and demands
  a per-target confirmation even under full access; jarvis serve --api
  serves read-only /health without the grant and requires it for /tools
  and /chat (each authorized turn lands in the ledger); jarvis overlay
  --attach refuses without it.
- Hard prohibitions, test-enforced: scan alone performs ZERO installs;
  wrong phrases, expired grants, foreign fingerprints, and tampered
  ledger entries are all refused or detected.
- Barge-in (Mark XXVII item): while speaking, the mic is monitored and
  the user talking over the reply stops the speech (pure detector
  _barge_triggered + monitor thread; silent degradation without a mic).
- Briefing continuity: the morning briefing recaps the most recent
  recorded session (title + turns).
